README.md:58:Each row is one match: where it is (`file`, `lineStart`, `lineEnd`), what it is (`nodeKind`: function, class, method, interface, type alias and so on), what holds it (`parent`), its signature, the lines in it that matched (`snippet`), and its `rank` and `finalScore`. Output uses [TOON](https://github.com/toon-format/spec), a table format that costs fewer tokens than JSON.
README.md:62:The `snippet` is the lines of the block that matched, each with its line number and cut at 160 characters. It shows up to three, then counts the rest (`...and 2 more matching lines`). A line is left out when the signature column already shows all of it, wherever it is in the block (the lines of a signature that spans several, or the signature line below a Rust item's attributes or doc comments), so the snippet is `null` when the signature is the only match. A line that says more than the signature stays in, such as a decorator, the start of an arrow function's body or a body on the same line. To read the whole block, open the file at `lineStart`–`lineEnd`. Under the table, `help` lines report files that were skipped, degraded (syntax errors, too deep to parse) or read lossily (invalid UTF-8), a partial result, hit lines that were cut, and a query with no positive terms.
